<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#incense">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Incense</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Incense is aromatic resin or plant material burned for its fragrance, especially in religious rituals. Frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a> from north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> were highly valued in the ancient world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a> took part in trade in incense and other luxury goods of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/incense.jpg" alt="Incense" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: nakimusi · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Incense_burner_at_Yakuo-in.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word incense comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> incendere, to burn. The earliest documented use is associated with ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>: excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qustul">Qustul</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, found one of the earliest known incense burners, of ceramic, dating to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-group-culture">A-Group culture</a> of about 3300 to 3000 BCE, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Early Dynastic period. Its decoration, including processional imagery read by some scholars as royal, suggests that incense already had ritual and political meaning.</p><p>In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> it was burned to mask unpleasant odours, repel malevolent spirits and honour the gods, and resin balls in prehistoric tombs at El Mahasna show its early place in funerary practice. Reliefs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a> depict expeditions to obtain incense and aromatic resins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> used it in prayers and offerings to oracles, and its use passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Incense is aromatic plant material that releases fragrant smoke when burnt, made from local ingredients: sage and cedar among the indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, agarwood and sandalwood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It comes in two main kinds. Indirect-burning incense, such as the resins frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a>, needs a separate heat source such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>.</p><p>Direct-burning incense is a paste of ground fragrant material and an odourless binder, shaped into sticks, coils or cones, which smoulders once lit. Coils burn for hours or days and are common in Chinese cultures, while cones were invented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the 1800s. Trade in incense materials formed a major part of commerce along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and the Incense Route.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Incense has served practical, aesthetic and religious ends. Its strong scent covered the smell of decay in funerals. It is offered to deities in Jewish, Christian, Taoist and Buddhist worship, and many cultures have seen the rising smoke as prayer directed to a deity on high. The incense of the Jewish Temple in Jerusalem, called ketoret, is stated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a> to be a mixture of stacte, onycha, galbanum and frankincense.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, calibrated incense sticks and incense clocks came with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and the poet Yu Jianwu (487–551) wrote of telling the night's hours by burning incense. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the art of incense appreciation is called kōdō, and in many Arab countries scented chips called bakhoor are burned to welcome guests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Incense” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Baekje craftsmen were famous: the tomb of King Muryeong at Gongju, found intact in 1971, yielded gold crown ornaments, jewellery and Chinese ceramics, and the gilt-bronze <b>incense</b> burner discovered at Neungsan-ri in 1993 is among the masterpieces of East Asian metalwork.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The most important was eaglewood, the one product Marco Polo mentioned; in 1155 a Cham envoy shipped 55,020 catties, about 33 tons, of <b>incense</b> to the Song court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At that time the old <b>incense</b> kingdoms of Saba, Qataban, Hadramawt and Ma'in still dominated south Arabia, and the overland caravan trade on which they had grown rich was beginning to lose ground to the sea route through the Red Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade routes from Arabia through the Negev brought <b>incense</b> and spices, and fortresses such as Arad guarded them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Saba rested on two foundations: irrigated agriculture and the <b>incense</b> trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade linked the African interior with Egypt and the Mediterranean world: gold, ivory, ebony, <b>incense</b>, exotic animals and skins were exchanged for luxury and manufactured goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
